---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Pipeline from "../components/Pipeline.astro";
import TriangulationDiagram from "../components/TriangulationDiagram.astro";
import ErrorBudget from "../components/ErrorBudget.astro";
import { links } from "../config";

const description =
  "How UAP Finder works: on-device detection and filtering, metadata-only uplink, 50 ms stereo matching across nodes up to 80 miles apart, triangulation with an explicit error model, 3D tracks, and clips requested only after a confirmed match.";

const toc = [
  ["overview", "Overview"],
  ["on-device", "On the node"],
  ["uplink", "Metadata uplink"],
  ["matching", "Matching"],
  ["triangulation", "Triangulation"],
  ["accuracy", "Accuracy"],
  ["tracks", "Tracks and flags"],
  ["clips", "Clips"],
  ["published", "What is published"],
  ["limitations", "Limitations"],
];

const packet = `{
  "type": "telemetry",
  "node_id": "4f9a2c1e-7b3d-4e8a-9c6f-1a2b3c4d5e6f",
  "gps_coordinates": {"latitude": 33.6292, "longitude": -112.3679, "altitude_m": 357.2},
  "imu_orientation": {"pitch_deg": 45.0, "roll_deg": 0.0, "yaw_deg": 0.0},
  "timestamp_ns": 1782345600000000000,
  "vector_target": {"azimuth_deg": 142.521, "elevation_deg": 65.104},
  "pixel_metrics": {"angular_velocity_deg_s": 12.4, "relative_intensity": 0.85},
  "seq": 1042,
  "track_id": "t-000173",
  "anomaly_score": 0.91,
  "sig": "<64 hex characters: HMAC-SHA256 with the node's key>"
}`;
---
<Base title="How it works" description={description}>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1>From a moving dot to a measured 3D track</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Video is analysed where it is recorded. Only small metadata packets travel to a shared server, which pairs
        simultaneous sightings, intersects their sight lines and reports every position with its uncertainty.
      </p>
      <nav aria-label="On this page">
        <ul class="toc">
          {toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section id="overview" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="overview-title">Overview</h2>
        <p>
          Each <strong>node</strong> is a small computer (an NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano) in a volunteer's home with a
          sky-facing camera, a GPS receiver for position and precise time, and optionally an orientation sensor. The
          <strong>server</strong> is shared. The design keeps the heavy data, the video, on the node, and moves only
          what triangulation needs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Pipeline />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="on-device" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="ondevice-title">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 1</p>
      <h2 id="ondevice-title">On the node: detect, track, filter</h2>
      <p>
        The camera streams video (by default 1920 × 1080 at 60 frames per second through a lens with a field of view
        of about 90°) into a pre-allocated buffer in memory that holds the last 10 seconds. A detector compares each
        frame with a slowly updated model of the background sky and picks out small moving points; a tracker links
        them from frame to frame into local tracks. A <strong>sky mask</strong> ignores everything below a minimum
        elevation (3° by default) and any pixels the host marks as rooftops, trees or neighbouring buildings.
      </p>
      <p>Before anything is reported, each confirmed track goes through false-positive filters:</p>
      <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="On-device filters">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Filter</th><th scope="col">What it looks for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Stars and planets</th><td>Objects moving at or below the sidereal rate (0.0042° per second) and fixed lights.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Clouds</th><td>Large, diffuse, low-contrast structure that is slow or drifts with the wind field measured across the frame.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Birds and insects</th><td>Objects close to the lens: large apparent size plus rapid growth or shrinking, a periodic wingbeat flicker (2–25 Hz) or defocus.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Aircraft</th><td>Tracks that follow an aircraft broadcasting its position by ADS-B, projected to each frame time, from a local receiver or an online feed.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Satellites and meteors</th><td>Off by default: one camera cannot tell a straight-flying unknown from a satellite. Triangulated altitude settles it on the server.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Filtered tracks are not sent. Their label and the reason are logged on the node, so an operator can audit what
        was suppressed and why. A track that has manoeuvred is never labelled as a satellite or meteor.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each detection's pixel position is converted to a direction in the sky (azimuth from true north and elevation
        above the horizon) using the lens calibration and the camera's pointing. Pointing accuracy dominates the
        error budget, so fixed cameras are calibrated against the star field; an orientation sensor, if fitted, is
        used to notice that a mount has moved, not to measure direction.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="uplink" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="uplink-title">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 2</p>
      <h2 id="uplink-title">Metadata-only uplink</h2>
      <p>
        For each surviving track the node sends a <strong>telemetry</strong> packet ten times a second for as long as
        the object is visible, plus a <strong>heartbeat</strong> every 10 seconds with its health and camera geometry.
        A packet holds the capture time in nanoseconds from a GPS-disciplined clock, the node's position, the camera's
        pointing, the direction to the object and a few image measurements. It is signed with a key unique to the
        node, so the server can reject forged, stale (more than 10 seconds old) or replayed messages.
      </p>
      <pre aria-label="Example telemetry packet"><code>{packet}</code></pre>
      <p class="small muted">
        Example from the <a href={links.protocolDoc}>wire protocol</a>. Serialised compactly and signed, a packet like
        this is about 520 bytes. Packets travel over UDP to a self-hosted server or over HTTPS in batches.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          The packet contains the node's exact GPS position because triangulation needs it. The server uses it
          internally; public responses round node positions to two decimal places, about 1 km.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="matching" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="matching-title">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 3</p>
      <h2 id="matching-title">Matching simultaneous sightings</h2>
      <p>
        The server keeps a spatial index of active nodes (heard from in the last 60 seconds). For every incoming
        sighting it looks for sightings from <strong>other nodes within 80 miles</strong> whose timestamps fall
        <strong>within 50 milliseconds</strong>. Fifty milliseconds matters: an object at Mach 6 covers about 100 m in
        that time, so when the neighbour has samples of the same track on both sides, the server interpolates its
        sight line to the exact moment of the packet.
      </p>
      <p>A candidate pair is accepted as the same object only if:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the two sight lines converge in front of both cameras, not behind one of them;</li>
        <li>the angle between them (the parallax) is at least 1°, so distance is meaningfully constrained;</li>
        <li>both ranges (150 m to 400 km) and the altitude (−500 m to 150 km) are physically plausible;</li>
        <li>
          the lines pass within a distance consistent with the expected angular noise: at most three times its
          predicted spread plus 30 m for timing and GPS error.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Competing candidates are ranked by how small their miss distance is relative to that prediction.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="triangulation" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="tri-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split">
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="eyebrow">Step 4</p>
          <h2 id="tri-title">Triangulation</h2>
          <p>
            Two sight lines in three dimensions almost never intersect exactly. The server finds the shortest segment
            between them; its midpoint is the position estimate and its length, the <em>error margin</em>, is a
            direct check on consistency.
          </p>
          <p>
            With three or more nodes the server solves a weighted least-squares problem: the point that minimises the
            squared perpendicular distances to all sight lines, with each line weighted by 1/(range × σ)², so that
            distant observers, whose sideways error is larger, count for less. The result is converted to latitude,
            longitude and height above the WGS84 ellipsoid.
          </p>
          <p class="small muted">
            Derivations and tests: <a href={links.mathDoc}>docs/MATH.md</a> and the shared
            <code>skynet_common.triangulation</code> module.
          </p>
        </div>
        <TriangulationDiagram id="hiw-tri" caption="Angles exaggerated for clarity." />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="accuracy" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="acc-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Step 5</p>
        <h2 id="acc-title">The error model, and why calibration and spacing matter</h2>
        <p>
          Each sight line constrains the position in the two directions perpendicular to it, with a standard
          deviation of range × σ, where σ is the angular error. Combining all lines gives a covariance for the
          position; the API reports its square-root trace as <code>sigma_m</code>, the 3D RMS error in metres. A
          Monte-Carlo test confirms that simulated errors match this prediction.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ErrorBudget />
      <div class="grid grid-3 takeaways">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Pointing calibration matters most</h3>
          <p>
            A 1080p sensor behind a 90° lens covers about 0.047° per pixel, and finding a point's centre gives
            0.01–0.05°. A consumer orientation sensor manages about 1°, which is 20 to 100 times worse. That is why
            fixed cameras are calibrated against the stars.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Spacing of 5–40 miles is the sweet spot</h3>
          <p>
            Too close and the sight lines are nearly parallel for distant objects. Too far and the object sits low on
            both horizons, where refraction, haze and obstructions dominate: at 80 miles it is only 8° up.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Timing has to be GPS-grade</h3>
          <p>
            Nodes discipline their clocks with the GPS pulse-per-second signal. Ordinary internet time over a home
            connection is only good to a few milliseconds, and the capture delay of each camera is calibrated once.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted not-modelled">
        Not modelled yet: atmospheric refraction (about 0.5° at the horizon, under 0.02° above 45° elevation), residual
        lens distortion and the node's own GPS position error (about 2–5 m, negligible next to angular error at these
        ranges).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tracks" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="tracks-title">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 6</p>
      <h2 id="tracks-title">3D tracks, speed, acceleration and flags</h2>
      <p>
        Every node labels its own tracks, so successive triangulations that share a node's track are linked into a
        single 3D track. Individual fixes are noisy (tens of metres at typical ranges), so velocity and acceleration
        are never taken from point-to-point differences. Instead the server fits a straight line and a parabola to
        the last second of points, and again to a once-per-second history of the last 30 seconds, and keeps each
        parameter's standard error.
      </p>
      <p>
        Flags use the <strong>2σ lower bound</strong> of the fitted value, so measurement noise alone cannot trigger
        them. The published maximum acceleration is that lower bound.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Track classifications">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Flag</th><th scope="col">Rule</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>hypersonic</code></th><td>Speed above 1,715 m/s (Mach 5 at sea level).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>high_g</code></th><td>Acceleration above 98 m/s² (10 g).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>ballistic</code></th><td>For at least 5 s, moving at 100 m/s or more with an acceleration that matches gravity (within the larger of 3σ and 2 m/s²) and is known to within 3 m/s².</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>stationary</code></th><td>Speed below 2 m/s for 30 s.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>unknown</code></th><td>The default when no rule has fired.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout callout--star">
        <p>
          <strong>A flag describes motion; it does not identify anything.</strong> Meteors, for example, routinely
          exceed the hypersonic threshold: they enter the atmosphere at roughly 11–72 km/s and burn up around 80–120 km
          altitude, and that altitude is what identifies them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="clips" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="clips-title">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 7</p>
      <h2 id="clips-title">The clip back-signal</h2>
      <p>
        Only when <strong>two or more nodes confirm a match</strong> does the server send each contributing node a
        signed <code>clip_request</code> for the 10 seconds around the event (5 seconds either side). Because the node
        keeps the most recent seconds of video in memory, the moments before the request can still be saved.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>The node verifies the request's signature and copies the frames out of memory to its local SSD.</li>
        <li>
          It encodes them to H.264 at low priority, on the processor rather than a hardware encoder (the Orin Nano does
          not have one), so detection is never starved.
        </li>
        <li>
          It uploads the file to a one-time upload address and records a per-frame timestamp file alongside it for
          forensic and stereo re-analysis.
        </li>
        <li>The server links the clip to the track, where it is available from the public track record.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="small muted">
        The design target for delivering the request is under 20 ms on the UDP transport. By default nodes also keep
        short clips of their own detections on their own disk, for up to 14 days, and upload nothing unless asked.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="published" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="pub-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="pub-title">What is and isn't published</h2>
        <p>The map and the public API are read-only and open to anyone. This is what they contain.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Published</h3>
          <ul class="checklist checklist--yes">
            <li>3D tracks: positions, altitude, speed, acceleration lower bound, flags and the uncertainty of each fix.</li>
            <li>Each fix's sight lines (azimuth and elevation) and which nodes contributed.</li>
            <li>Nodes: optional label, position rounded to about 1 km, camera pointing and field of view, online status and basic health.</li>
            <li>Clips requested after a confirmed match, once uploaded, linked from the track.</li>
            <li>Network statistics, the stereo coverage map, and volunteer counts per 1° × 1° cell with at least 3 volunteers.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Not published</h3>
          <ul class="checklist checklist--no">
            <li>Continuous video. It stays on the node.</li>
            <li>Detections filtered out on the node (stars, clouds, birds, insects, ADS-B aircraft).</li>
            <li>Exact node coordinates, which the server uses only for triangulation.</li>
            <li>Volunteer names, email addresses, notes, postal codes and individual locations.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted pub-note">
        Rounding is a safeguard, not a guarantee: published sight lines combined with triangulated positions could, in
        principle, narrow a node's location further. This is under review before launch. See the
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limitations" class="section anchor-section" aria-labelledby="lim-title">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Read this</p>
      <h2 id="lim-title">Honest limitations</h2>
      <ul class="limits">
        <li>
          <strong>The network is new.</strong> The accuracy figures on this site are predictions of the error model,
          checked in simulation. They have not yet been verified in the field against targets of known position.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Most detections will be ordinary.</strong> Not every aircraft broadcasts ADS-B, and ADS-B data can be
          missing or delayed, so some aircraft will get through. So will balloons, drones, satellites, meteors and
          insects.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cameras only see what stands out against the sky.</strong> Unlit objects at night, small or distant
          objects by day and anything behind cloud are invisible. Coverage depends on weather, daylight and where
          hosts live.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Low elevations are hard.</strong> Near the horizon, refraction (about 0.5°, not yet modelled), haze
          and obstructions degrade accuracy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>False matches are possible.</strong> In a busy sky, two nodes may see different objects whose sight
          lines happen to cross. Gating and a third node reduce this but cannot rule it out; two-node fixes deserve
          more caution than three-node ones.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Systematic errors are not in the error bars.</strong> A mount that shifts, a mis-set clock or an
          uncalibrated capture delay biases every measurement from that node until it is fixed. The error model assumes
          random errors.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Short tracks give weak kinematics.</strong> Acceleration in particular needs many points. Because
          flags are conservative, a real but marginal event may go unflagged.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The software is pre-release.</strong> Version 0.1; some hardware paths, such as the orientation-sensor
          driver, have not yet been tested on real devices.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No identification.</strong> The system measures where something was and how it moved. It does not
          say what it was.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .takeaways {
    margin-top: 28px;
  }

  .not-modelled,
  .pub-note {
    margin-top: 20px;
    max-width: 90ch;
  }

  .limits {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .limits li {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  pre code {
    white-space: pre;
  }
</style>
